{"parentUuid":"2aa1e925-cb81-4b5f-a857-e8bc39e9c905","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"97157054-4481-4ee5-8a30-c8c035f10cd0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a2ef058c2e7a6e634</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_016CSTQsKeN6UCQ7V6hFz5zp</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-home-demo-code-links-issue-tracker/09b3c611-9c10-46a6-8270-9fa48daf676b/tasks/a2ef058c2e7a6e634.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"/code-review medium 615\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I found no correctness bugs in PR 615. There are three low-severity findings: one is a misleading error message and two are doc claims that no longer match the code.\n\nThe main change checks out. In \u0060transitionLeaf\u0060 (\u0060internal/cli/cli.go:1735\u0060), \u0060resolveActor()\u0060 now runs once, before \u0060buildAction(actor)\u0060, so the assignee \u0060lit start\u0060 writes and the actor its event records are the same value. The other transition commands ignore the new actor argument. Nothing outside the tests still calls \u0060lit start --assignee\u0060.\n\nThe ReportFindings tool isn't available in this session, so the findings are listed here only.\n\n1. **\u0060/home/demo/code/links-issue-tracker/internal/cli/flagset.go:163\u0060** (low): the new \u0060retiredFlags[\"assignee\"]\u0060 entry applies to every command, not just \u0060lit start\u0060. Any command that doesn't register \u0060--assignee\u0060 now refuses it with exit 3 (\u0060unsupported_flag\u0060) and the message \"--assignee does not name who is\n… 6 more lines"},"uuid":"3956891b-9a72-4d1e-ad63-012b64b6704a","timestamp":"2026-10-13T20:53:10.043Z"}
{"parentUuid":"feb117d9-f80f-44b4-b3b0-feb0a2a63b93","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_011Cfzu9iPXAJhazewm3rgxM","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-5-5","stop_reason":"tool_use","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":""}],"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2518}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"84444161-adb1-4b60-84bb-3d2290a949c4","timestamp":"2026-10-13T20:53:29.867Z"}
{"parentUuid":"84444161-adb1-4b60-84bb-3d2290a949c4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_011Cfzu9iPXAJhazewm3rgxM","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-5-5","stop_reason":"tool_use","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The medium review found three low-severity issues and no bugs. All three hold up against the code:\n\n1. **The \u0060--assignee\u0060 pointer fires on every command.** That includes read commands like \u0060show\u0060 and \u0060export\u0060, where telling someone to \"name yourself with \u0060--by\u0060\" is nonsense. The problem is that the \u0060retiredFlags\u0060 table is global, while a retired flag belongs to the command it was retired from. The fix: each flag set declares its own retired flags. \u0060registerActor\u0060 (the one place that adds \u0060--by\u0060) declares the retired \u0060--assignee\u0060, and \u0060next\u0060 declares \u0060--continue\u0060. The pointer then appears only on commands that accept \u0060--by\u0060, and the data sits next to the flag it replaces. This narrows \u0060--continue\u0060 too: it used to hit \u0060lit start --continue\u0060 with \"run \u0060lit next\u0060\", a pointer that only ever made sense on \u0060next\u0060.\n2. **The import help is wrong about which YAML files accept \u0060--by\u0060.** A create-only YAML file also refuses it. I'll fix the wording after checking the test.\n3. **cli-reference has two stale lines.** The transition shape needs \u0060--by\u0060 as a shared flag, and the \"unknown-flag parse error\" sentence needs the exception."}],"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2518}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"e9687283-ffbd-4b7e-8332-5e9d42c68670","timestamp":"2026-10-13T20:53:33.514Z"}
