{"parentUuid":"01ea250c-1f99-481a-a0d0-9cd1be3143e5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_011Cg36UwLh3tCGvgVCkTJdC","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-5-5","stop_reason":"end_turn","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two things are running in the background: the CI test of the reporter, and the local suite. The nightly test run takes about eight minutes, then the report job runs. I'll check back when either finishes.\n\n\u0060★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────\u0060\n- lit keeps its store in the git remote itself, under \u0060refs/dolt/data\u0060. So a CI job that can push the repo can also file a ticket, with no separate service. The catch is that embedded Dolt runs \u0060git\u0060 against its own cache repo (\u0060.dolt/git-remote-cache/<sha>/repo.git\u0060), so the token \u0060actions/checkout\u0060 puts in the checkout's config never reaches that push. That's why the action sets a *global* \u0060extraheader\u0060 and the checkouts use \u0060persist-credentials: false\u0060, which avoids sending two Authorization headers.\n- Splitting the report into its own job with \u0060needs:\u0060 + \u0060if: always() && needs.X.result != 'success'\u0060 does two things. It narrows \u0060contents: write\u0060 to the few lines that need it, so the test suite never holds a write token. And it replaces the old \u0060failure() || cancelled()\u0060 trick, because a job's \u0060result\u0060 already reads \u0060cancelled\u0060 when the job hit its timeout.\n- The verification is a real failure, not a staged one. The branch's first commit leaves the token list stale on purpose, so the dispatched run goes red for the same reason master's nightly does.\n\u0060─────────────────────────────────────────────────\u0060"}],"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":453}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"afc3b2d1-5d4c-4b80-bd2c-a7ca11132108","timestamp":"2026-10-15T00:43:03.770Z"}
